{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Google Cloud Platform?

**Google Cloud Platform (GCP), now presented as Google Cloud, is a public cloud platform.**
It lets teams create compute, storage, database, networking, analytics,
machine-learning, and operations resources through APIs. A team places resources in
projects and locations, connects them through networks and service APIs, and pays for
reserved or measured use.

GCP matters because managed services can remove work such as hardware procurement,
control-plane maintenance, elastic placement, and some replication. They do not remove
the need to choose ownership, identity, data, failure, capacity, and recovery contracts.

The core invariant is: **Google operates the cloud platform; the workload team owns the application's user-visible outcome.**
A green resource status is not proof that requests are correct, authorized, affordable,
or recoverable.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Start with the resource hierarchy and owner

The resource hierarchy is the management structure that answers where policy is
inherited, who can change a workload, and which project carries its APIs, quotas, and
billing. It is separate from the runtime request path.

{% system-flow title="Google Cloud governance flows from organization to service resource" caption="Attach a control at the highest scope where it is true for every descendant. Keep exceptions narrow, owned, and reviewable." %}
{% system-node title="Organization" eyebrow="Enterprise root" icon="users" tone="violet" %}
Represents the company. Organization Policy, IAM, security evidence, and administrative
controls can apply across every descendant.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Folders" eyebrow="Delegation boundary" icon="layers" tone="blue" %}
Group projects by environment, business unit, application family, or regulatory scope.
Use them to delegate ownership without granting organization-wide access.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Projects" eyebrow="Workload boundary" icon="boxes" tone="cyan" %}
Contain service resources and their enabled APIs, IAM bindings, quotas, labels, and
billing association. Separate production when it needs an independent blast radius.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Service resources" eyebrow="Runtime capability" icon="server" tone="green" %}
VMs, Cloud Run services, GKE clusters, buckets, topics, datasets, and databases carry
their own location, access, capacity, lifecycle, and recovery behavior.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Make the hierarchy operational

- Give every project an accountable team, environment, data classification, cost
  center, and deletion policy.
- Separate shared networking and security projects only when the platform team can
  publish availability, capacity, change, and incident contracts for them.
- Apply broad policy in audit mode first; provide a supported remediation path before
  denying deployments.
- Track application dependencies across projects. Resource hierarchy alone does not
  show the complete service graph.

Review the current [Google Cloud resource hierarchy](https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy)
before designing inheritance or project boundaries.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Trace a production request through the data plane

A public request crosses global and regional infrastructure, but management actions use
a separate control plane. During an incident, ask whether users can complete the
journey before asking whether a deployment API is healthy.

{% system-flow title="A governed request through Google Cloud" caption="Carry one request identity, deadline, and trace context across the path. Put retries at the layer that understands whether the operation is safe to repeat." %}
{% system-node title="Cloud Load Balancing and Cloud CDN" eyebrow="Global edge" icon="globe" tone="blue" %}
Terminates public traffic, applies edge policy, serves eligible cached content, and
selects a healthy backend. Health checks must represent the journey they protect.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Cloud Armor and admission" eyebrow="Policy boundary" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Filters abusive traffic and enforces protocol, identity, quota, and request-size rules
before expensive application work begins.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Cloud Run, GKE, or Compute Engine" eyebrow="Regional compute" icon="server" tone="violet" %}
Executes bounded business logic with a workload identity, explicit timeouts, controlled
concurrency, and autoscaling that respects downstream capacity.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Managed data and messaging" eyebrow="Authoritative state" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Cloud SQL, Spanner, Firestore, Bigtable, Cloud Storage, and Pub/Sub provide different
transaction, consistency, location, replay, and recovery contracts.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Cloud Monitoring and audit evidence" eyebrow="Feedback plane" icon="activity" tone="amber" %}
Metrics, logs, traces, audit events, deployment versions, and business outcomes reveal
user impact and support a specific operator action.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

The **control plane** creates and changes resources. The **data plane** serves requests
and data. An existing workload can sometimes keep serving during a control-plane issue,
while a successful deployment can still create a broken data plane.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Choose compute by the control the workload needs

Prefer the highest-level runtime that still satisfies the workload's isolation,
networking, startup, scaling, portability, and operational requirements.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Cloud Run" eyebrow="Serverless containers" icon="cloud" tone="green" %}
Fits stateless HTTP services, event handlers, and jobs that can use the platform's
request, concurrency, startup, scaling, and execution model. Bound retries and side
effects because instances can start and stop dynamically.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="GKE" eyebrow="Managed Kubernetes" icon="boxes" tone="violet" %}
Fits teams that need Kubernetes APIs, scheduling policy, sidecars, operators, or
multi-workload orchestration and can own workload, upgrade, network, and capacity policy.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Compute Engine" eyebrow="Virtual machines" icon="server" tone="blue" %}
Fits guest operating-system control, legacy software, specialized drivers, or custom
machine layouts. The team owns images, guest patching, process health, and fleet rollout.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Batch and managed jobs" eyebrow="Queued execution" icon="timer" tone="amber" %}
Fit bounded asynchronous work that can declare resources, checkpoint progress, and
retry safely without keeping an always-on request-serving fleet.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Do not select a runtime from the happy path alone. Test cold starts, rollout overlap,
connection limits, quota, accelerator availability, autoscaler delay, private egress,
and the loss of one declared placement slice. The current
[compute selection guide](https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/compute-area/choose-compute-options)
documents the platform's supported options.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Turn demand into a capacity and cost envelope

Cloud cost follows architecture. Peak requests drive serving capacity, average
requests drive monthly volume, cacheability changes origin work, response bytes drive
network delivery, and telemetry policy drives ingestion and retention.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="Peak x origin share" label="Origin demand" detail="Cache hits reduce origin work, not user demand" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="Demand / tested unit" label="Capacity floor" detail="Apply target utilization and deployment reserve" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="Requests x bytes" label="Variable volume" detail="Separate edge, origin, logs, and retained data" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="Total / business unit" label="Unit economics" detail="Track cost per request, tenant, job, or model result" tone="violet" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
The lab uses transparent teaching assumptions, not current Google Cloud prices. Replace
its rates with measured telemetry, current regional pricing, discounts, taxes, support,
and negotiated terms before making a budget or purchase decision.
{% /callout %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/gcp-cost" dataFile="/api/content/technology/gcp/data/workload-envelope.json" /%}

### Read the model in the right order

1. Measure peak and average demand separately; a monthly average cannot size a burst.
2. Benchmark sustainable work per capacity unit at the target tail latency and error
   rate, including real dependencies.
3. Reserve capacity for rollout, warm-up, retry bursts, and a failed unit before buying
   a commitment.
4. Validate regional quota for both normal deployment and recovery, including CPUs,
   addresses, load balancers, accelerators, database connections, and API limits.
5. Reconcile actual spend by project, workload, environment, owner, and business unit.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/gcp/code/size-regional-service.py" language="python" title="Size a zonal service for one-zone loss" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Design for failure domains, not product labels

A **failure domain** is a resource or group of resources that can fail together. A
zone is an infrastructure failure domain inside a region. A second region is a separate
recovery design, not a checkbox: it needs routing, serving capacity, data authority,
dependencies, IAM, quota, and a tested promotion and failback procedure.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/technology/gcp/data/failure-domain-topology.json" /%}

### Keep four promises separate

- **Availability:** can a healthy target accept a new request now?
- **Durability:** which acknowledged writes remain recoverable after the incident?
- **Recovery point objective (RPO):** how much recent state may be lost?
- **Recovery time objective (RTO):** how long until routing, capacity, state, and
  dependencies restore the required journey?

A backup protects historical data; it is not live serving capacity. A replica can
improve availability; it can also copy a valid deletion or corruption. Use isolated
retention, restore testing, and application-level validation alongside replication.
Google's [reliability building blocks](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/infra-reliability-guide/building-blocks)
describe regions and zones as design boundaries.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Select data services from the contract

Start with the authoritative data model, transaction boundary, access path, consistency,
latency, scale, location, retention, replay, RPO, and RTO. Then select a service.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Cloud Storage" eyebrow="Objects and artifacts" icon="storage" tone="blue" %}
Use for objects addressed by key: media, exports, logs, model artifacts, and backups.
Choose location, lifecycle, retention, versioning, access, and recovery intentionally.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Cloud SQL" eyebrow="Managed relational" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Use when MySQL, PostgreSQL, or SQL Server semantics fit. Model connection limits,
maintenance, high availability, read replicas, backups, and regional recovery.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Spanner" eyebrow="Scalable relational" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Use when relational transactions and horizontal scale justify its schema, key,
placement, consistency, and operational model. Do not choose it only for the word global.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Firestore or Bigtable" eyebrow="Non-relational access" icon="braces" tone="cyan" %}
Choose document or wide-column semantics from query shape, key distribution,
transaction needs, hotspot risk, consistency, and retention rather than avoiding SQL.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Pub/Sub" eyebrow="Asynchronous delivery" icon="repeat" tone="amber" %}
Decouple producers and consumers with explicit acknowledgement, retry, ordering,
dead-letter, replay, idempotency, and backlog-age policies.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="BigQuery and Vertex AI" eyebrow="Analytics and ML" icon="gauge" tone="rose" %}
Build governed analytical and model workflows with partitioned data, lineage, quality
checks, evaluation, versioned artifacts, endpoint objectives, monitoring, and rollback.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

For asynchronous work, acknowledge only after the durable side effect commits. Redelivery
must not apply that side effect twice.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/gcp/code/idempotent-event-handler.py" language="python" title="Make a redelivered event harmless" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Apply the platform to real workload shapes

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Public transactional API" eyebrow="Low-latency journey" icon="globe" tone="blue" %}
Use edge policy and caching, regional compute, an authoritative relational store, and
Pub/Sub for non-blocking follow-up. Track tail latency, error rate, saturation, payment
or order correctness, and retry amplification.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Streaming analytics" eyebrow="Event-time pipeline" icon="activity" tone="amber" %}
Use Pub/Sub for ingestion, Dataflow for bounded or streaming transforms, Cloud Storage
for durable landing, and BigQuery for governed analysis. Track backlog age, late data,
duplicate handling, schema changes, and replay cost.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Production ML service" eyebrow="Model lifecycle" icon="workflow" tone="violet" %}
Use governed data in Cloud Storage or BigQuery, Vertex AI for training and registry
workflows, and a versioned serving path. Gate releases on quality, safety, latency, cost,
drift, fallback behavior, and rollback evidence.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

In every shape, keep the system of record explicit. Caches, search indexes, analytical
tables, features, and model outputs are derived state unless the business contract says
otherwise.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card tone="warn" %}
## Secure and operate the workload as one system

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Issue short-lived identity" label="Authenticate" %}
Federate people and external workloads. Attach a dedicated identity to each runtime and
avoid downloadable service-account keys where a managed identity path exists.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Authorize the exact path" label="Control" %}
Grant narrow roles on named resources, constrain network ingress and egress, protect
secrets and keys, and review inherited IAM and Organization Policy together.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Measure user outcomes" label="Observe" %}
Correlate service-level indicators, dependency saturation, quota, deployment version,
audit events, and cost with one request or business operation.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Rehearse recovery" label="Respond" %}
Inject instance, zone, dependency, credential, data, and regional failures; verify
routing, capacity, authority, restore, reconciliation, and safe failback.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Production review checklist

- Name the project, region, zones, data locations, network paths, and accountable owner.
- Use workload identity and short-lived credentials; alert on service-account key use
  and privileged policy changes.
- Define SLOs from user journeys, then alert on actionable error-budget consumption and
  dependency pressure rather than resource health alone.
- Set budgets and anomaly alerts, export detailed billing data, label resources, and
  review unallocated spend.
- Treat quotas as capacity limits. Monitor consumption and approve increases before
  launches or recovery exercises.
- Provision through reviewed infrastructure code, detect drift, use progressive
  rollout, and retain a tested rollback path.
- Record data classification, encryption, retention, deletion, backup, restore, RPO,
  RTO, and residency decisions for every authoritative dataset.

See the current [workload identity guidance](https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/workload-identities)
when removing long-lived keys.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
A credible GCP design states who owns it, where every request and data copy travels,
what survives each failure, how identity is issued, which quota constrains recovery,
how cost scales, and what evidence permits rollback or failover.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

